<sdf version="1.9">
  <!--
    Textron Systems Fleet-class Common Unmanned Surface Vessel (CUSV) —
    a real, currently-active US Navy mine-countermeasures / anti-submarine
    warfare USV. Sourced physical data (Wikipedia's "Fleet-class unmanned
    surface vessel" page, itself sourced to Textron/naval-technology.com):
    displacement 7.7 tonnes (7700 kg), length 12 m (39 ft), beam 3.4 m
    (11.2 ft), semi-planing monohull, top speed 35 kn, described as
    similar in scale/role to a large rigid-hulled inflatable boat (RHIB).

    DISCLOSED ASSUMPTION: Textron has not published CUSV's propulsion
    configuration (engine count/type, waterjet vs screw). This model
    assumes a twin differential-thrust layout (waterjet or twin-screw),
    consistent with the sourced "semi-planing monohull, patrol-boat-like
    role" description and with every other surface vehicle in this
    project (WAM-V, BlueBoat) using twin differential thrust. This is a
    simulation-only assumption, not a claim about the real vehicle's
    actual engine room — stated here so it's disclosed up front, not
    discovered later.

    DISCLOSED SIMPLIFICATION: the hydrodynamics plugin below (the same
    gz-sim-hydrodynamics-system used for WAM-V/BlueBoat) is a simple
    linear+quadratic drag model with no planing-lift term — it cannot
    reproduce a real planing hull's drag reduction at speed. This
    simulation does NOT attempt to reach the real vehicle's 35 kn top
    speed; thrust/drag are tuned instead for stable, controllable
    low-speed maneuvering (a few m/s) sufficient for this project's attack
    tests (arm, hold, GPS/AIS/C2 response) — the same "controllable for
    testing, not full-envelope-realistic" bar every other vehicle in this
    project is held to. Mass, displacement, and dimensions are real and
    sourced; top-speed performance is explicitly not reproduced.

    Buoyancy/collision box: a monohull semi-planing form is NOT the same
    shape as WAM-V/BlueBoat's twin-pontoon catamaran boxes, so this is not
    copy-scaled from either — a single box sized smaller than the full
    12x3.4m envelope (8.0 x 2.8 x 0.625 m) to account for bow/stern taper
    (a real hull's underwater volume is well under its full length x beam
    x depth) and freeboard above the waterline. At 1000 kg/m^3 water
    density, 7700 kg displaced = ~55% of this box's 0.625 m height
    submerged at equilibrium (~0.34 m draft) — a physically plausible
    draft for a lightweight aluminium/composite semi-planing hull this
    size, and close enough to the exact 7.7 m^3 displacement volume to
    avoid the shallow-draft numerical edge case an earlier, larger
    (10.0x3.2x1.0, ~24% submerged) box hit live (see the base_link CG
    comment below for what that looked like and how it was diagnosed).
    Final float trim confirmed live (see vehicle_twins/*/README.md), same
    iterative-tuning process BlueROV2's own model.sdf documents.

    Inertia (ixx/iyy/izz): box approximation from mass + collision box
    dimensions, same formula docs/NEW_AUV_QUICKSTART.md documents:
    ixx=m/12*(h^2+w^2), iyy=m/12*(l^2+w^2), izz=m/12*(l^2+h^2).

    Hydrodynamics drag terms: BlueBoat's own live-tuned coefficients
    (30 kg boat), scaled by mass_ratio^(2/3) = (7700/30)^(2/3) ~= 40.4 —
    the standard dimensional-analysis heuristic for how drag scales with
    wetted area for geometrically-similar-ish bodies, since the raw
    quadratic-drag coefficients in this simple plugin aren't independently
    derivable from displacement alone. Sanity-checked: twin 4000N-max
    thrusters against the scaled quadratic drag term settle at ~2.8 m/s
    (~5.5 kn) cruise, ~1.0 m/s^2 acceleration — comfortable, controllable
    numbers for a 7.7-tonne vessel, not a toy-boat or a rocket. Subject to
    live retuning in Phase A4 exactly like every other vehicle here.

    No CAD/mesh data is available for CUSV — visual geometry is a plain
    primitive hull-shaped box, not a claim of accurate rendering. Physics
    (mass/buoyancy/drag/thrust), not visual fidelity, is what's under test.
  -->
  <model name="cusv">
    <link name="base_link">
      <!-- Revised geometry (2026-08-08, second iteration): the original
           box (10.0x3.2x1.0, only ~24% submerged at equilibrium) produced
           a stable 38.5 deg pitch tilt confirmed via live ArduPilot ATTITUDE
           reports AND the raw Gazebo world pose (both agreed, so it was a
           genuine physics result, not a frame-convention artifact) —
           consistent with a shallow-draft box being a numerically
           sensitive edge case for gz-sim-buoyancy-system's box-submersion
           approximation. Reduced footprint (8.0x2.8) and increased height
           (0.625) so ~55% of the box is submerged at equilibrium instead
           of ~24% — closer to the real 7700 kg / 1000 kg/m^3 = 7.7 m^3
           displacement (14.0 m^3 box vs the original 32 m^3), a smaller,
           better-conditioned margin above the exact displacement volume.

           CG (separately) is still deliberately well below the collision
           box's geometric center, for the same top-bottom-symmetry reason
           documented in the original fix: confirmed live that CG at the
           box's own centroid floats stably CAPSIZED (roll=180, verified
           via live ATTITUDE messages, not a frame artifact).

           Result after this revision, confirmed live: roll=0.0 deg
           (perfectly level, stable, not oscillating), pitch settles to a
           steady ~7.2 deg static trim (also stable, not oscillating) —
           a modest, plausible resting trim (real vessels commonly sit
           with a few degrees of static trim depending on load
           distribution), not a broken float. Not chased to exactly 0.0:
           the residual is too small to trace to a specific single cause
           (the aft thruster mass offset alone is ~1.4 cm from the vessel
           center, far too small via simple static torque to explain even
           7 deg against this hull's righting stiffness) and is most
           likely inherent to the simplified box-buoyancy approximation at
           this submersion fraction, not a modeling mistake with an
           obvious fix. -->
      <inertial>
        <pose>0 0 -0.45 0 0 0</pose>
        <mass>7700.0</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>5281.3</ixx>
          <ixy>0</ixy>
          <ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>46097.3</iyy>
          <iyz>0</iyz>
          <izz>41317.3</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>

      <collision name="hull_collision">
        <pose>0 0 -0.2 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box><size>8.0 2.8 0.625</size></box>
        </geometry>
      </collision>

      <visual name="hull_visual">
        <pose>0 0 -0.2 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box><size>8.0 2.8 0.625</size></box>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <diffuse>0.25 0.27 0.29 1</diffuse>
          <ambient>0.2 0.22 0.24 1</ambient>
          <specular>0.4 0.4 0.4 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>
      <!-- small deckhouse, purely cosmetic, for visual orientation -->
      <visual name="deckhouse_visual">
        <pose>1.2 0 0.35 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <box><size>2.0 1.6 0.8</size></box>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <diffuse>0.6 0.62 0.64 1</diffuse>
          <ambient>0.5 0.52 0.54 1</ambient>
          <specular>0.4 0.4 0.4 1</specular>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>

    <link name="motor_stbd_link">
      <!-- Rotation -90 0 90 (degrees) matches BlueBoat's own proven
           motor_stbd_link pose exactly (sim_config/models/blueboat/model.sdf)
           — REQUIRED, not cosmetic: gz-sim-thruster-system generates thrust
           along the joint's own local -Z axis, so without this rotation the
           thrust vector points straight down/up in the world frame instead
           of along the hull's forward/aft axis. This was missing from
           CUSV's original build (confirmed live 2026-08-09 while
           investigating a reported "vehicle deviating from the goal /
           speed too slow" symptom): AUTO nav showed the correct target
           bearing the whole time, throttle correctly ramped toward 100%
           chasing WP_SPEED, but groundspeed stayed near-zero and heading
           drifted instead of converging — exactly what unrotated
           (vertical) thrust looks like, not a navigation-math bug. -->
      <pose degrees="true">-3.6 -1.3 -0.45 -90 0 90</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>15.0</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.5</ixx>
          <ixy>0</ixy>
          <ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>0.5</iyy>
          <iyz>0</iyz>
          <izz>0.5</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <visual name="thruster_visual">
        <geometry>
          <cylinder><radius>0.15</radius><length>0.4</length></cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <diffuse>0.1 0.1 0.1 1</diffuse>
          <ambient>0.1 0.1 0.1 1</ambient>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>
    <joint name="motor_stbd_joint" type="revolute">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>motor_stbd_link</child>
      <axis>
        <xyz>0 0 -1</xyz>
        <limit>
          <lower>-1e16</lower>
          <upper>1e16</upper>
        </limit>
        <dynamics>
          <damping>0.05</damping>
        </dynamics>
      </axis>
    </joint>

    <link name="motor_port_link">
      <!-- Same fix, same reasoning as motor_stbd_link above. -->
      <pose degrees="true">-3.6 1.3 -0.45 -90 0 90</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>15.0</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.5</ixx>
          <ixy>0</ixy>
          <ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>0.5</iyy>
          <iyz>0</iyz>
          <izz>0.5</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <visual name="thruster_visual">
        <geometry>
          <cylinder><radius>0.15</radius><length>0.4</length></cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <diffuse>0.1 0.1 0.1 1</diffuse>
          <ambient>0.1 0.1 0.1 1</ambient>
        </material>
      </visual>
    </link>
    <joint name="motor_port_joint" type="revolute">
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <child>motor_port_link</child>
      <axis>
        <xyz>0 0 -1</xyz>
        <limit>
          <lower>-1e16</lower>
          <upper>1e16</upper>
        </limit>
        <dynamics>
          <damping>0.05</damping>
        </dynamics>
      </axis>
    </joint>

    <!-- sensors -->
    <link name="imu_link">
      <inertial>
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <mass>0.1</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.0001</ixx>
          <ixy>0</ixy>
          <ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>0.0001</iyy>
          <iyz>0</iyz>
          <izz>0.0001</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>
      <sensor name="imu_sensor" type="imu">
        <pose degrees="true">0 0 0 180 0 0</pose>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>1000.0</update_rate>
      </sensor>
      <sensor name="navsat_sensor" type="navsat">
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>10.0</update_rate>
        <topic>navsat</topic>
      </sensor>
    </link>
    <joint name="imu_joint" type="revolute">
      <child>imu_link</child>
      <parent>base_link</parent>
      <axis>
        <xyz>0 0 1</xyz>
        <limit>
          <lower>0</lower>
          <upper>0</upper>
        </limit>
        <dynamics>
          <damping>1.0</damping>
        </dynamics>
      </axis>
    </joint>

    <!-- plugins -->
    <plugin name="gz::sim::systems::JointStatePublisher"
      filename="gz-sim-joint-state-publisher-system">
    </plugin>
    <plugin name="gz::sim::systems::OdometryPublisher"
      filename="gz-sim-odometry-publisher-system">
      <odom_frame>odom</odom_frame>
      <robot_base_frame>base_link</robot_base_frame>
      <dimensions>3</dimensions>
    </plugin>

    <plugin name="gz::sim::systems::Thruster"
      filename="gz-sim-thruster-system">
      <namespace>cusv</namespace>
      <joint_name>motor_port_joint</joint_name>
      <thrust_coefficient>-0.02</thrust_coefficient>
      <fluid_density>1025</fluid_density>
      <propeller_diameter>0.3</propeller_diameter>
      <velocity_control>0</velocity_control>
      <use_angvel_cmd>0</use_angvel_cmd>
    </plugin>

    <plugin name="gz::sim::systems::Thruster"
      filename="gz-sim-thruster-system">
      <namespace>cusv</namespace>
      <joint_name>motor_stbd_joint</joint_name>
      <thrust_coefficient>0.02</thrust_coefficient>
      <fluid_density>1025</fluid_density>
      <propeller_diameter>0.3</propeller_diameter>
      <velocity_control>0</velocity_control>
      <use_angvel_cmd>0</use_angvel_cmd>
    </plugin>

    <!-- Linear+quadratic drag, BlueBoat's live-tuned coefficients scaled
         by mass_ratio^(2/3) ~= 40.4 (see header comment for derivation
         and sanity check). -->
    <plugin filename="gz-sim-hydrodynamics-system"
            name="gz::sim::systems::Hydrodynamics">
      <link_name>base_link</link_name>
      <water_density>1000.0</water_density>
      <xDotU>0</xDotU>
      <yDotV>0</yDotV>
      <zDotW>0</zDotW>
      <kDotP>0</kDotP>
      <mDotQ>0</mDotQ>
      <nDotR>0</nDotR>
      <xU>-808</xU>
      <yV>-1615</yV>
      <zW>-1615</zW>
      <!-- Angular damping (kP/mQ/nR + abs variants) is NOT the same
           mass^(2/3) scale as the translational terms above. BlueBoat's
           box (0.9x0.55x0.30) and CUSV's box (10.0x3.2x1.0) are not
           geometrically similar — CUSV's box grew ~11x in length but
           only ~40x in mass, so a mass-only scaling badly under-damps
           rotation for this much longer, flatter hull. Confirmed live
           (2026-08-08): mass^(2/3)-scaled angular damping produced a
           continuously rolling/pitching vessel that never settled
           (observed quaternion oscillating through ~60-70 degree swings
           over multiple seconds, not decaying). Increased 100x from the
           mass-scaled values below empirically until it settled level —
           this is a live-tuned value, not a first-principles derivation,
           same as every other vehicle's hydrodynamics in this project. -->
      <kP>-24200</kP>
      <mQ>-24200</mQ>
      <nR>-32300</nR>
      <xUabsU>-1010</xUabsU>
      <yVabsV>-2423</yVabsV>
      <zWabsW>-2423</zWabsW>
      <kPabsP>-40400</kPabsP>
      <mQabsQ>-40400</mQabsQ>
      <nRabsR>-48500</nRabsR>
    </plugin>

    <!-- ardupilot plugin -->
    <plugin name="ArduPilotPlugin"
      filename="ArduPilotPlugin">
      <fdm_addr>127.0.0.1</fdm_addr>
      <fdm_port_in>9100</fdm_port_in>
      <connectionTimeoutMaxCount>5</connectionTimeoutMaxCount>
      <lock_step>1</lock_step>
      <gazeboXYZToNED degrees="true">0 0 0 180 0 90</gazeboXYZToNED>
      <modelXYZToAirplaneXForwardZDown degrees="true">0 0 0 180 0 0</modelXYZToAirplaneXForwardZDown>
      <imuName>imu_link::imu_sensor</imuName>

      <!--
        port motor — SERVO1_FUNCTION 73 (ThrottleLeft)
        COMMAND type computes cmd = multiplier * (normalized_input + offset);
        normalized_input in [0,1], offset=-0.5 -> effective range [-0.5,0.5].
        multiplier=8000 gives max +-4000N per thruster at full deflection,
        matching the twin-4000N sanity check in the header comment (BlueBoat
        uses the identical formula with multiplier=70 -> +-35N per side).
      -->
      <control channel="0">
        <jointName>motor_port_joint</jointName>
        <multiplier>8000</multiplier>
        <offset>-0.5</offset>
        <servo_min>1000</servo_min>
        <servo_max>2000</servo_max>
        <type>COMMAND</type>
        <cmd_topic>/model/cusv/joint/motor_port_joint/cmd_thrust</cmd_topic>
      </control>

      <!--
        stbd motor — SERVO3_FUNCTION 74 (ThrottleRight)
      -->
      <control channel="2">
        <jointName>motor_stbd_joint</jointName>
        <multiplier>8000</multiplier>
        <offset>-0.5</offset>
        <servo_min>1000</servo_min>
        <servo_max>2000</servo_max>
        <type>COMMAND</type>
        <cmd_topic>/model/cusv/joint/motor_stbd_joint/cmd_thrust</cmd_topic>
      </control>
    </plugin>

  </model>
</sdf>
